'''Kadarism''', known during its founder's lifetime as '''Burawi Socialist Theory''', is a {{W|democratic socialism|democratic socialist}} ideology developed by [[Manaa Kadar]], the first [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]]. It combines {{W|vanguardism}} with a two-stage theory of international socialist revolution and rejects the {{W|Marxism|Marxist}} conception of {{W|communism}} as a final stage of development, holding instead that {{W|democratic socialism}} — in which the state exists to serve the people and the people determine the form of that service through democratic institutions — is the permanent and final form of political organisation.

Kadarism was the official ideology of the [[Northern and Central Socialist Party]] (NCSP) and its successor, the [[Kadarist Party]], which governed [[Burawa]] from 1932 until the [[1962 Crisis]]. In the modern era, the [[Party for Expansion of Socialism]] (PES) articulates a revised form known as '''neo-Kadarism'''. Kadarist parties exist in numerous countries across [[Abyala]], [[Kaftia]], [[Fosia]], and [[Illypnia]].

== Core tenets ==

=== Two-stage vanguard theory ===

The central element of Kadarism is the two-stage theory of socialist revolution, which Kadar developed after observing the {{W|communist}} revolution in [[Daras]] in 1906:

# '''First stage''': A vanguard composed of educated citizens — not necessarily the existing elite, but those capable of acquiring political power within established institutions — transforms capitalist states with existing industrial capacity into socialist republics. The presence of accumulated capital is essential: revolutions cannot succeed in states without an industrial base, because there is no capital to seize and redirect toward the common good.
# '''Second stage''': Those socialist states, having inherited the industrial capacity and international influence of their capitalist predecessors, fund and support revolutions in territories that lack existing capital or liberal political structures.

The theory holds that states in which capital is concentrated in very few hands, or in which no significant industrial base exists, cannot generate socialist revolutions internally — they require the intervention of established socialist powers.

=== Rejection of communism ===

Unlike {{W|Marxism–Leninism}}, which posits the eventual {{W|withering away of the state}}, Kadarism holds that socialism is the permanent and final form of political organisation. The state does not wither; it is perfected. Kadar argued that democratic socialism — in which the state exists to serve the people and the people determine the form of that service through elections and participatory institutions — represents the endpoint of political development, not a transitional phase toward a stateless communist society.

This position distinguishes Kadarism from orthodox Marxist movements and has been a source of tension between Kadarist and communist parties in countries where both operate.

=== Vanguard principle ===

Kadarism holds that effective revolution requires the participation of educated citizens who can navigate existing power structures. This position contains an implicit elitism that Kadar did not disavow: workers alone, in Kadar's view, lacked the institutional knowledge and access necessary to seize a modern state. The vanguard is drawn from the educated classes but is not identical with the existing elite — it comprises those who recognise the injustice of the existing system and are willing to use their position to transform it.

=== Atonement principle ===

A distinctive element of Kadarism is the principle that capitalists who recognise the injustice of the existing system and are willing to atone — by surrendering their privileges and contributing to the socialist project — can be forgiven and reintegrated into the socialist state. Kadar articulated this principle in a treatise written after the death of his uncle [[Markos Dhuxul]], a prominent industrialist, in 1911. The principle may have been influenced by the [[Lathrian]] theology of forgiveness practised by Kadar's mother.

The atonement principle had practical consequences: the [[Mazar Agreement]] of 1936 granted amnesty and property rights to capitalists who defected to the republican cause during the [[Great Adonian War]].

=== Anti-colonialism ===

Kadarism incorporates a strong anti-colonial principle. Kadar regarded the retention of territories against the will of their populations as incompatible with socialist ideology. This principle was embodied in the [[Act of Self-Determination]] of 1934, which granted certain regions of Burawa the choice to leave the republic.

== History ==

=== Development under Manaa Kadar ===

Kadar developed the core elements of Burawi Socialist Theory during the first decades of the twentieth century, drawing on his observations of industrial conditions in [[Burawa]], his military service in the [[Second War of the Isthmus]], and his visit to [[Daras]] in 1906. The ideology guided the [[Northern and Central Socialist Party]] (NCSP) through its rise to power and the establishment of the [[Socialist Republic of Burawa]] in 1932.

During Kadar's lifetime, the ideology was referred to as '''Burawi Socialist Theory''' or simply "the theory." Kadar himself did not attach his name to it.

=== Kadarist Party (1945–1962) ===

After Kadar's death in 1945, leadership of the NCSP passed to [[Siciid Dhegey]], who reconstituted the party as the '''[[Kadarist Party]]''' and formally attached Kadar's name to the ideology. Under Dhegey, the party pursued the internationalist programme of the two-stage theory with particular emphasis on the second stage: exporting socialism in coordination with [[Daras]] and [[Balisca]].

The Kadarist Party's implementation of the theory prioritised international objectives over domestic welfare. Wages stagnated, housing deteriorated, and Burawa's diplomatic isolation from non-socialist neighbours deepened. The party supported revolutionary movements of varying ideological coherence, applying the principle that any revolution supported by foreign vanguards could produce a socialist outcome.

This approach contributed to the [[1962 Crisis]], a period of political and economic instability that ended Kadarist Party governance.

=== 1962 Crisis and fragmentation ===

The 1962 Crisis prompted [[Kunciil Hanad]], who had represented the liberal wing of the NCSP, to found the [[Socialist Party of Burawa]]. Hanad's party drew on the NCSP's founding principles while rejecting the authoritarian and internationalist excesses of the Kadarist period. The Kadarist Party fragmented, and its successor organisations were absorbed into the modern party system.

=== Neo-Kadarism ===

{{main|Party for Expansion of Socialism}}

The [[Party for Expansion of Socialism]] (PES), founded in the post-1962 political landscape, articulates a revised form of Kadarism known as '''neo-Kadarism'''. Neo-Kadarists retain the vanguard principle and the two-stage framework but modify the second stage:

* '''Original Kadarism''': The second stage consists of supporting revolutions led by foreign vanguards — any revolution with vanguard leadership is considered a viable candidate for support.
* '''Neo-Kadarism''': The second stage consists of funding education to cultivate vanguards within target societies, who will then carry out the first stage autonomously. Direct support for revolutions is rejected in favour of long-term vanguard cultivation.

Neo-Kadarists argue that the original formulation led the Kadarist Party to support ideologically incoherent movements at considerable fiscal cost. The revised approach prioritises the development of educated vanguard cadres over immediate revolutionary intervention.

== International influence ==

Kadarist parties and movements exist in numerous countries outside Burawa. The ideology's design for export — its explicit purpose of extending socialism to states that lack the conditions for indigenous revolution — has given it international reach.

* '''[[Abyala]]''': Kadarist parties operate in several Abyalan nations, where the two-stage theory has been applied to contexts lacking established industrial bases.
* '''[[Kaftia]]''': Kadarist movements exist across the continent.
* '''[[Fosia]]''': Kadarist parties are present in parts of the continent, though their influence is smaller than in Abyala and Kaftia.
* '''[[Illypnia]]''': Kadarist movements exist in limited numbers.

The international appeal of Kadarism derives in part from its rejection of communism as a final stage, which distinguishes it from Marxist–Leninist movements and makes it acceptable to populations wary of communist state models.

== Criticism ==

=== From the liberal socialist tradition ===

[[Kunciil Hanad]] and the liberal wing of the NCSP criticised Kadarism for its reliance on a selected elite to direct the state. Hanad argued that the vanguard principle concentrated power in the hands of a small group and risked alienating the broader population. The Hanad tradition emphasises decentralised governance, stronger protections for individual rights, and a less authoritarian approach to state management.

=== From communist movements ===

Communist parties criticise Kadarism for its rejection of communism as a final stage, arguing that Kadar's position represents a capitulation to liberal democratic forms and a failure to pursue the full transformation of society toward a stateless, classless condition.

=== From the right ===

Conservative and liberal critics argue that Kadarism's vanguard principle is inherently authoritarian, that its internationalist programme interferes in the sovereign affairs of other states, and that its economic policies lead to stagnation and inefficiency.

